74: 
75: Write $J_{aI}=\partial G_a/\partial A_I$. Each certificate cell records
76: the prime, all 126 coordinates, all 252 self-dual compact components,
77: the same ordered 81 graph identifiers, all entries of the $81\times126$
78: Jacobian, 81 pivot columns, and the determinant of that minor.
79: \input{tables/certificate_cells.tex}
80: The determinant is recomputed by Python-integer elimination independently
81: of the streaming rank sieve. Euler's identity
82: $\sum_I A_I J_{aI}=\deg(G_a)G_a$ is checked for every row.
83: 
84: If a saved determinant is nonzero modulo $p$, the corresponding integer
85: determinant polynomial is not identically zero. Its nonvanishing defines a
86: nonempty Zariski-open set in characteristic zero on which these 81
87: polynomials are algebraically independent. Invariance and the known generic
88: quotient dimension supply the matching upper bound. These are generic local
89: coordinates; neither global orbit separation nor polynomial generation of
90: the entire ring follows.
91: 
92: \section{Polynomial spaces and literature comparison}
93: The homogeneous-space dimensions through degree twelve are
94: $1,2,7,14,72$. Products must be included in value-space comparisons.
95: At degree twelve the ten product monomials are $I_4^3$, the three symmetric
96: products of sextics, and the six products $I_4 I_{8,j}$. The inherited
97: degree-twelve computation reaches rank 72 using these products and 62 graph
98: directions. Polynomial-space evidence uses gradients concatenated at several
99: points; the functional Jacobian uses one point. These are separate sieves.
100: Factor exponents in a Hilbert product are not automatically generator counts
101: at higher degrees when generators and relations may coexist.
102: 
103: The octic comparison uses a selection of six displayed literature
104: contractions: the first five expressions from equations (4.12)--(4.15) of
105: \cite{cederwall}, together with the hatted expression (4.18). The exact
106: machine-readable map includes the quartic-square product explicitly.
107: The $6\times6$ block compares primitive classes modulo products; any product
108: correction is retained in the full $7\times7$ matrix.
109: 
110: At degree ten the repository's implemented readings of the twelve published
111: candidates span a twelve-dimensional space whose intersection with the
112: two-dimensional product space has dimension one in the reconstructed
113: coordinate matrix. Thus a claimed invertible $12\times12$ identification
114: with the graph primitive complement would be incorrect for those readings.
115: The delivered map has twelve rows and fourteen atlas columns and records
116: the source-reading qualifications. Rational reconstruction and fresh modular
117: holdouts are strong checks on these maps, but finite samples alone do not
118: prove a characteristic-zero polynomial identity. These qualifications do not
119: affect the graph-only nonzero-minor proof.
120: 
121: \section{Reproduction and validation}
122: Use the declared dependencies and run the regression suite before evaluating
123: certificates. The original low-degree commit is preserved by
124: \texttt{v0.1-low-degree-complete}. The release commands are:
125: \begin{verbatim}
126: python scripts/graph_to_latex.py
127: python scripts/search_rank81.py
128: python scripts/verify_rank81.py
129: python scripts/verify_rank81.py --recompute
130: python scripts/validate_rank81_lorentz.py
131: python scripts/map_literature_basis.py --degree 8
132: python scripts/map_literature_basis.py --degree 10
133: \end{verbatim}
134: The default verifier checks the saved matrix and metadata; the explicit
135: \texttt{--recompute} mode repeats the graph evaluations. These modes are
136: reported separately. The search stage checkpoints each raw row and its
137: pivot, checks run identity on resume, and bounds worker submissions.
138: Degree-specific discovery remains available through the existing nauty
139: pipelines. \texttt{run\_degree.py} accepts additional edge-list JSONL streams
140: and separates polynomial completeness from functional rank.
141: 
142: Regression tests cover the 6D reproduction, self-duality,
143: the zero quadratic contraction, exact integer arithmetic, reverse
144: Jacobians against an independent oracle, graph parsing beyond ten vertices,
145: and genuine Lorentz boosts. The final graph selection is checked under both
146: a spatial rotation and a boost; neither uses floating-point tolerances.
